Subject: [PATCH] server: slots are parked on the RPC worker

With --slot-park-path DIR, POST /slots/{id}?action=save and action=restore
with {"filename": KEY} keep the slot's state on the RPC worker's own storage
under KEY (ggml-rpc/park.h) instead of in a file here, and action=park-list,
park-delete and park-status look after the keys. DIR holds only the
bookkeeping: per key a manifest, the tokens (4 bytes each) and the positions
of the parked attention cells (24 bytes each). "device": false in the body
keeps the old file behaviour when --slot-save-path is set too.

- A save writes the attention cells at positions the key does not hold yet
  and the recurrent state as it is: bytes in proportion to the tokens added,
  plus one recurrent state.
- A recurrent state cannot be rewound, so a request that returns and differs
  before the parked end needs an earlier state: the recurrent state of the
  save before, which is kept on the worker. With "checkpoint": true, and on a
  key's first save, the slot's own checkpoint is parked as well.
- The slot's tokens are compared with the parked ones. If they differ before
  the parked end, the files that end before the difference are kept and the
  rest written again ("mode": "rewrite"): a changed history is never resumed
  as it was.
- The manifest names the files with their sizes and checksums and is replaced
  only after the worker has the new files whole, so a save that dies leaves
  the previous state loadable. A restore checks sizes and checksums and leaves
  the slot empty, never half restored, when anything is wrong.
- Errors carry "park_error": park_pruned (410), park_torn and
  park_incompatible (409), park_no_space (507), park_unsupported (501),
  park_empty and park_bad_request (400), park_io (500). A key that was never
  parked is a 404.
- A save or a restore waits for its slot like a request sent to it, in the
  order they came (the task carries the slot's id), and lets what waited behind
  it go on when it is done. The server does one thing on a slot at a time, so
  no park action runs beside a request or beside another one.
- The tokens and cell positions of a key are appended to by a save that adds
  and written to a new pair of files by one that rewrites; the old pair, like
  the worker's old files, goes only after the manifest is replaced. A server
  that dies in any save leaves the key as its last finished save left it.
  LLAMA_PARK_DIE_AT=manifest ends the server just before the manifest is
  replaced, for the test of that.
- One RPC worker only, no draft model, no media in the slot.

